WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.790
Every character from The Walking Dead has
a fan following, and Carol is no exception.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:10.299
Watching her arc take her from domestic abuse
and zombie apocalypse survivor to gun-toting

00:00:10.299 --> 00:00:15.679
badass has been an incredible journey, driven
by some powerful performances from Melissa

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:16.679
McBride.

00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:20.580
She's proven she can play everything from
battered wife to trembling survivor to someone

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.830
capable of doing whatever it takes.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:23.960
And we mean whatever.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.100
"Just, just look at the flowers."

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.020
Ouch.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.800
There’s no doubt McBride has become part
of the heart and soul of the show.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.460
But, you’ve seen her before, too—even
if she looked so different that you might

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.399
not have actually realized it.

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:48.300
Born in Kentucky, McBride headed to Atlanta
in the 1980s to start what's been a wildly

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.489
random career.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:53.729
Throughout the 1990s, she appeared in a slew
of commercials that seem to have been lost

00:00:53.729 --> 00:00:58.510
to the mists of time, but footage of her first
major acting credit thankfully remains.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:03.780
She appeared in a 1993 episode of Matlock,
playing the fidgety Darlene Kellogg in a bizarre

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.040
episode called "Matlock's Bad, Bad, Bad Dream."

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.579
The story starts with Matlock meeting some
friends in a jazz nightclub and restaurant,

00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:15.460
and he's introduced to Darlene, who's craving
a cigarette but trying to quit.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.320
The rest of the episode is literally right
out of Matlock's nightmares, and when his

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:25.500
dreams transport him back to a Prohibition-themed
restaurant she shows up as a 1930s-era cigarette

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.500
girl.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.829
Her long, curly hair was the height of fashion
in the 1990s for sure, but it also makes her

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:31.990
look completely different.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.539
In an epic twist, it's completely possible
to imagine Season 1 Carol watching that exact

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:38.810
show while doing Ed's ironing.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.080
McBride still sported her '90s curls in her
next role, a small part in the long-running

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.250
southern cop drama In the Heat of the Night.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:50.750
Her role in the 1994 two-part episode "Give
Me Your Life," is admittedly brief.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:55.000
McBride plays a reporter determined to get
the story during an investigation into a shady

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.479
church in Mississippi.

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:00.549
While she's playing a pretty stereotypical
plucky news reporter, there are still some

00:02:00.549 --> 00:02:03.249
hints of the McBride we'd later come to know
and love.

00:02:03.249 --> 00:02:08.720
It only lasted a single season in 1995 before
being canceled, but the supernatural horror

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.080
mystery show American Gothic had some big
names attached, including executive producer

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.590
Sam Raimi, and a cast that included eventual
A-listers Evan Rachel Wood, Sarah Paulson,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.019
Lucas Black, and Gary Cole.

00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:25.790
McBride appeared in the episode “Dead to
the World," as the sheriff's onetime girlfriend

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.780
who disappeared under mysterious circumstances.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.239
Appearing in a flashback scene, Holly Gallagher
is an archetype familiar to McBride's fans,

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:36.150
the eager-to-please woman who thrives in her
caretaker role.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:40.200
She's said herself that it's something she
has in common with Carol, telling AMC,

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.360
"I've gotten myself into some difficult situations
because I couldn't say no.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:49.060
Actually, I've gotten myself into some difficult
places because I thought I could fix somebody."

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:54.030
After appearing in a handful of made-for-TV
movies with names like A Season in Purgatory,

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:59.370
Any Place But Home, and Close to Danger, McBride
landed in a two-part episode of Walker, Texas

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.459
Ranger alongside living internet meme Chuck
Norris.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:13.420
Again in a nurturing role, McBride played
Rachel Woods, a pediatric doctor tasked with

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.220
caring for a young patient, played by Haley
Joel Osment, who's been removed from a house

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:19.549
during a drug bust.

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:22.010
"Walker told me I have AIDS."

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.650
Yep, it's that episode.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.560
In the cult hit 1990s series Dawson’s Creek,
Dawson and his friends head to a bar in an

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.659
attempt to distract him from his broken heart.

00:03:30.659 --> 00:03:34.879
When he spots McBride's Nina from across the
bar, he knows he's found the one.

00:03:34.879 --> 00:03:39.129
Nina chats with Dawson a bit, and invites
him Dawson back to her place to hang out and

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:42.440
watch movies, which is the 90s version of
"Netflix and chill".

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.909
He passes, but they still share a kiss — making
McBride the focus of all kinds of fangirl

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:51.380
jealously long before anyone ever started
wanting Carol and Daryl to be a thing.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:00.379
When McBride was approached to audition for
the 2007 adaptation of Stephen King's The

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:02.440
Mist, she was initially reluctant.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:07.860
But, she went along, and eventually landed
a gig that would lead to her most iconic role.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:12.140
She not only got the role described only in
the credits as "Woman with Kids at Home,"

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.410
but she made such an impression on producer
Frank Darabont that when it came time to start

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.989
casting for Walking Dead a few years later,
he gave her a call.

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:24.271
It's absolutely not surprising, even though
she's only in a single, early scene and in

00:04:24.271 --> 00:04:25.870
the final moments of the movie.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.590
The ending to The Mist is one that sticks
with those that see it.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:33.060
She doesn't say anything, and she doesn't
have to—it's no wonder Darabont kept her

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.060
number.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:37.710
Even with Carol's increasing importance on
the Walking Dead, McBride has still found

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.110
time to do occasional work outside the show.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:46.560
In 2014, The Reconstruction of William Zero
debuted at the Fantasia Film Festival in Montreal.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.039
Described as a "suburban mad-scientist tale,”
McBride plays one of the scientist’s colleagues

00:04:51.039 --> 00:04:54.350
in a mind-bending drama that deals with some
pretty heavy material.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:58.530
The moral of the story suggests there's no
shortcut around grief, and that the only way

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:03.020
to get through it is to deal with it head
on… a message Carol would certainly approve

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.020
of.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.020
Thanks for watching!

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.430
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.580
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!`

